of the complexity, severity and unmanageability of the evolving symptom picture.  Eventually, the patient would become completely incapacitated, bedfast, and die, the process taking about three to five years on average.  

While retrospective studies have found earlier instances of what may have been the same disorder, the first clearly identified examples of paresis among the insane were described in Paris after the [[Napoleonic Wars]].  General paresis of the insane was first described as a distinct disease in 1822 by [[Antoine Laurent Jesse Bayle]].  General paresis most often struck people (men far more frequently than women) between twenty and forty years of age. By 1877, for example, the superintendent of an asylum for men in New York reported that in his institution this disorder accounted for more than twelve percent of the admissions and more than two percent of the deaths.

Originally, the cause was believed to be an inherent weakness of character or constitution.   While [[Johannes Friedrich August von Esmarch|Esmarch]] and [[Jessen]] had asserted as early as [[1857]] that [[syphilis]] caused general paresis, progress toward the general acceptance by the medical community of this idea was only accomplished later by the eminent nineteenth-century syphilographer [[Alfred Fournier]] ([[1832]]-[[1914]]).  In [[1913]] all doubt about the syphilitic nature of paresis was finally eliminated when [[Hideyo Noguchi|Noguchi]] and [[Moore]] demonstrated the syphillitic [[spirochaete]]s in the brains of  paretics.  

In 1917 [[Julius Wagner-Jauregg]] discovered that infecting paretic patients with [[malaria]] could halt the progression of general paresis. He won a [[Nobel Prize]] for this discovery in 1927. After [[World War II]] the use of [[penicillin]] to treat syphilis made general paresis a rarity:  even patients manifesting early symptoms of actual general paresis were capable of full recovery with a course of penicillin.  The disorder is now virtually unknown outside [[Third World]] countries, and even there the epidemiology is substantially reduced.  

See also: [[Tuskegee experiment]]

[[Category:Psychiatry]]

[[de:Parese]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>San Francisco State University</title>
    <id>149780</id>
    <revision>
      <id>42100075</id>
      <timestamp>2006-03-03T20:57:38Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>207.145.50.174</ip>
      </contributor>
      <comment>/* Notable Alumni */</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''San Francisco State University''' is a branch of the [[California State University]] system.  It is located in the southwestern part of [[San Francisco, California]] near [[Lake Merced]], [[Fort Funston]], and only a mile north of [[Daly City]].

In the autumn of [[2003]], approximately 29,686 students were enrolled in the university, of which 73.7% were [[undergraduate]] students and 26.3% were [[Graduate_student|graduate]] students.

{{CSU_taxobox |
  seal_image=[[Image:Sfsu seal0001.gif|center|San Francisco State University Seal]] |
  moto= ''Experientia Docet''|
  establishment=[[1899]] |
  president=Robert A. Corrigan |
  location=[[San Francisco, California]] |
  undergrad=21,000 |
  grad=7,000 |
  faculty=1,646 |
  campus=[[Urban area|Urban]], 106 acres (0.4 km&amp;sup2;)|
  mascot=Gators |
  website=www.sfsu.edu
}}

==Academics==
The university's colleges are:

* Behavioral and Social Sciences, 
* Business, 
* Creative Arts, 
* Education, 
* Ethnic Studies, 
* Health and Human Services, 
* Humanities, 
* Interdisciplinary Studies 
* Science and Engineering.

In addition, the university features an extended learning program. 

The university awards bachelor's degrees in 116 areas of specialization and master's degrees in 95. It jointly offers two doctoral programs; a doctorate in education in partnership with [[UC Berkeley]] for aspiring principals and school administrators, and a doctorate in physical therapy with [[University of California, San Francisco|UC San Francisco]].

==History==
*[[1899]] - Founded as San Francisco State Normal School
*[[1901]] - First graduating class
*[[1921]] - SF Normal School became the SF State Teachers College
*[[1923]] - First bachelor of arts degree awarded
*[[1935]] - Renamed San Francisco State College
*[[1966]] - Beginning of the era of campus riots led by university-subsidized organizations, including the [[Students for a Democratic Society]], the [[Black Students Union]], and the [[Third World Liberation Front]] and marked by counter-protests and widespread charges of corruption and election fraud in the student newspaper.
*[[1972]] - Received University status as California State University, San Francisco
*[[1974]] - Renamed San Francisco State University
*[[1999]] - Celebrated 100th birthday

==Athletics==
The school's athletic teams, called the Gators, compete in the [[California Collegiate Athletic Association]] (except in [[wrestling]], that is in the [[Rocky Mountain Athletic Conference]]), in the [[Division II]] of the [[National Collegiate Athletic Association|NCAA]]. SFSU fields eleven sports for men and women for the [[autumn|fall]], [[winter]], and [[Spring (season)|spring]] seasons. Fall sports for men include [[cross country running|cross country]] and [[Football (soccer)|soccer]]. Fall sports for women include cross country and soccer. Winter sports for men include [[basketball]] and wrestling. Winter sports for women include basketball and indoor [[track and field]]. The spring sport for men is [[baseball]]. Spring sports for women include outdoor track and field and [[softball]].

SFSU has produced three major league baseball players two later became All-Stars (former Met shortstop Bud Harrelson, and former Brewers and Red Sox outfielder Tommy Harper).

==Controversy==

The campus is known for having a diverse and activist student body.  In 1968,  what was then the longest [http://www.sfsu.edu/~100years/textonlycent/time/longtime.htm#1968 student strike] in the nation's history, resulted in establishment of  the creation of a School of Ethnic Studies, and increased recruiting and admissions of minority students.

More recent controversies have included questions of [http://www.sfsu.edu/~news/announce/105.htm racial profiling] surrounding the arrest of Antwi Akom, assistant professor of Africana Studies, student [http://www.sfsu.edu/~news/announce/102.htm protests] of [[counter-recruitmetnt|military recruiters on campus]], and a confrontation between students of [http://www.sfsu.edu/~news/announce/101.htm different political views]. The [[Campus Antiwar Network]] (CAN) has charged that the university is involved in monitoring campus political groups. A protest organized by CAN also appeared on the [[Pentagon]]'s database of anti-war protests it monitored that was leaked to NBC in December 2005. The University's extensive and sustained efforts at addressing [http://www.sfsu.edu/~news/sfsuresp.htm tensions] between Pro-Israel and Pro-Palestinian students in 2002 have become a national model for addressing civil discussion and disagreement on college campuses.

==Notable Alumni==
*[[Annette Bening]] - [[actor|actress]]
*[[Dean Biersch]] - founder of the [[Gordon Biersch Brewing Company]]
*[[Po Bronson]] - [[author]] and [[journalist]] ([[Master of Fine Arts|MFA]] in [[creative writing]])
*[[Willie Brown (politician)|Willie Brown]] - member and 58th Speaker of the [[California State Assembly]] and former mayor of [[San Francisco, California]]
*[[John Burton]] - president [[pro tempore]] of the [[California State Senate]]  
*[[David Carradine]] - [[actor]] (best known for the television series ''[[Kung Fu (TV series)|Kung Fu]]'')
*[[Dana Carvey]] -  [[comedian]] (best known for his characterizations in [[Saturday Night Live]] and in ''[[Wayne's World]]'')
*[[Margaret Cho]] - Comedienne
*[[Stephen Colletti]] - ''[[Laguna Beach: The Real Orange County]]'' cast member (dropped out)
*[[Peter Coyote]] - actor, author
*[[Saeb Erekat]] - Controversial official of the Palestinian Authority
*[[Jennifer Finch]] - former [[bassist]] for all-girl punk group [[L7 (band)|L7]]
*[[Gregory Fischbach]] - founder of [[Acclaim Entertainment]], a [[videogame]] company
*[[Heather Fong]]  - current and first female chief of the [[San Francisco Police Department]] ([[Master's degree|MA]])
*[[Ben Fong-Torres]] - writer, broadcaster, editor at ''[[Rolling Stone]]''
*[[Ernest J. Gaines]] - novelist
*[[Andreas Glocker]] - founder of [[Sirius Connections]]
*[[Danny Glover]] - ''[[Lethal Weapon]]'' actor
*[[Charles Hall]] - inventor of the [[waterbed]]
*[[Delroy Lindo]] - actor
*[[Johnny Mathis]] - [[musician]]
*[[Manny Mashouf]] - founder of [[bebe stores|bebe stores inc.]] clothing retail shops
*[[Stan Mazor]] - while at [[Intel]], helped to design the first [[microprocessor]] with [[Federico Faggin]]
*[[Frances Mayes]] - author of [[Under the Tuscan Sun.]]
*[[Greg Proops]] - actor and [[stand-up comedian]]
*[[Anne Rice]] - novelist
*[[Terry Riley]] - composer
*[[Mario Savio]] - [[Berkeley, California|Berkeley]] free speech [[activist]]
*[[Ronnie Schell]] - actor, comedian
*[[Jeffrey Tambor]] - actor
*[[Bill Thomas]] -  American politician, representing the 22nd District of California in the House of Representitives
*[[Cal Tjader]] - [[jazz]] vibraphonist
*[[Jens Vindum]] - Herptastic Collections Manager California Academy of Sciences
*[[B.D. Wong]] - actor
*[[Leland Yee]] - [[California State Assembly]] speaker pro tempore
*[[Dan 'The Automator' Nakamura]] - Music Producer
*[[Janet Weiss]] - drummer [[Sleater-Kinney,Quasi]]
*[[Fat Mike]] - Lead Singer for the punk band [[NOFX]]

==External links==
*[http://www.sfsu.edu/ Official website]
*[http://athletics.sfsu.edu/ Official athletics website]
*[http://www.sfgate.com/cgi-bin/article.cgi?f=/c/a/2005/11/02/AKOM.TMP  SFGate: S.F. State teacher arraigned in arrest incident]
*[http://xpress.sfsu.edu/archives/news/004800.html The Golden Gate XPress: Black Studies Professor Arrested]

