  <page>
    <title>Dirk Diggler</title>
    <id>149838</id>
    <revision>
      <id>40110170</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-18T05:06:09Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>24.252.50.80</ip>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Dirk Diggler''' (born approx. 1960) is a fictional [[porn star]] of the [[1970s]] who featured in [[Paul Thomas Anderson]]'s 1997 movie ''[[Boogie Nights]]'' (played by [[Mark Wahlberg]]). Diggler's first appearance was in Anderson's short film ''The Dirk Diggler Story''. The character of Dirk Diggler is loosely based on real-world porn star [[John Holmes (actor)|John Holmes]].

{{spoiler}}

Diggler, (whose real name is Eddie Adams) was discovered in the [[San Fernando Valley]] as a teen by adult movie producer Jack Horner (played in ''Boogie Nights'' by [[Burt Reynolds]]).

Diggler's life at home wasn't very pleasant, so he moved in with Horner and his wife, adult film star Amber Waves. Later on, Dirk and Amber had an affair.

Dirk became a huge star during the 1970s, his name reaching the mainstream and his face appearing on the cover of [[Time Magazine]], among others. Diggler's movies were very popular, possibly due to his 13-inch penis. He also got to be with a lot of actresses off-camera, in addition to the ones he had sex with for the purpose of his films. But with all that money, fame and lifestyle came a dark side too: he had to endure a suicide by one of his co-workers, and he began dabbling in drugs.

Dirk tried at one point during the late '[[1970s|70s]] to turn his porn industry success into a music career, but soon after entering the music studio, he gave up on that idea.

By the early [[1980s]], Dirk's drug habit as well as his arrogance almost ruined him. He wasn't in high demand by adult film producers because they had newer, younger actors who they thought could become popular too. As a consequence, Dirk lost almost all of his money and he began wandering the streets, trying to [[hustle]] to earn an income before finally pulling his act together.

His fate is unknown after the events of ''Boogie Nights''.

----
'''Dirk Diggler''' is also the nickname of Dallas Mavericks basketball player Dirk Nowitzki.

In the video game &quot;[[Diablo II]]&quot;, there is an [[easter egg (virtual)|easter egg]] related to this character. One of the items that can be found by the player's in-game personality is a dirk known as &quot;The Diggler&quot;.

[[Category:Film characters|Diggler, Dirk]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>In C</title>
    <id>149839</id>
    <revision>
      <id>39530331</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-14T02:53:28Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Cmdrjameson</username>
        <id>101935</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>Compact allmusic URL</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{ Album infobox | &lt;!-- See Wikipedia:WikiProject_Albums --&gt;
| Name        = In C
| Type        = [[Album (music)|Album]]
| Artist      = [[Terry Riley]]
| Cover       = Terry Riley-In C (album cover).jpg
| Background  = orange
| Released    = [[October 1]], [[2000]]
| Recorded    = Jul 8, 1985-Jun 12, 1997
| Genre       = [[Avant-Garde]]
| Length      = 55:38
| Label       = [[Atma]]
| Producer    = ?
| Reviews     = &lt;nowiki&gt;&lt;/nowiki&gt;
* [[All Music Guide]] (3/5) [http://www.allmusic.com/cg/amg.dll?p=amg&amp;sql=10:55r928oi052a link]
| Last album = '''''[[You're No Good]]''''' &lt;br /&gt; (2000)
| This album = ''In C'' &lt;br /&gt; (2000)
| Next album = '''''[[Requiem for Adam]]''''' &lt;br /&gt; (2001)
}}
'''''In C''''' is an [[aleatoric music|aleatoric]] [[musical piece]] composed by [[Terry Riley]] in [[1964]] for any number of unspecified performers, but preferably 35 or more. As its title suggests, it is in the [[key (music)|key]] of C, the simplest key to perform on the [[piano]]. It is a response to the abstract academic [[serialism|serialist]] techniques used by [[composer]]s in the mid-[[twentieth century]] and is often cited as the first [[minimalist music|minimalist]] composition.

''In C'' consists of 53 short, numbered musical phrases; each phrase may be repeated an arbitrary number of times. Each [[musician]] has control over which phrase he or she plays: players are encouraged to play the phrases starting at different times, even if they are playing the same phrase. The performance directions state that the [[musical ensemble]] should try to stay within two to three phrases of each other. The phrases must be played in order, although some may be skipped. As detailed in some editions of the score, it is customary for one musician (&quot;traditionally played by a beautiful girl,&quot; Riley notes) to play the [[note]] C (in [[octave]]s) in repeated [[eighth note]]s.  This drone functions as a [[metronome]] and is referred to as &quot;The Pulse.&quot;

''In C'' has no set duration; performances can last as little as fifteen minutes or as long as several hours, although Riley indicates &quot;performances normally average between 45 minutes and an hour and a half.&quot;

The piece begins on a C [[major chord]] (patterns one through seven) with a strong emphasis on the [[mediant]] E and the entrance of the note F which begins a series of slow progressions to other chords suggesting a few subtle and ambiguous changes of [[key (music)|key]], the last pattern being an alteration between Bb and G. Though the [[polyphonic]] interplay of the various patterns against each other and themselves at different rhythmic displacements is of primary interest, the piece may be considered [[heterophonic]].

Recordings of the piece include:
* State University Center of Creative and Performing Arts (1968), Sony 7178
*[[Shanghai Film Orchestra]] conducted by [[Wang Yongji]] (1989), Celestial Harmonies 13026
*''In C'' 25th Anniversary Concert (recorded 1990, released 1995), New Albion 71.
*[[Bang on a Can]] (1998/2000), Cantaloupe 46243.
*[[Acid Mothers Temple]] &amp; The Melting Paraiso U.F.O. (2002), Squealer 37.

==External link==
*[http://www.otherminds.org/shtml/Scores.shtml OtherMinds.org Scores: In C] by Terry Riley

[[Category:Minimalistic compositions]]
[[Category:Aleatory compositions]]</text>
    </revision>
