    <title>Brighouse</title>
    <id>149854</id>
    <revision>
      <id>42097527</id>
      <timestamp>2006-03-03T20:35:45Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>82.38.131.92</ip>
      </contributor>
      <comment>/* Buildings and renovations */</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">&lt;table align=&quot;right&quot;&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;{{infobox England place with map|
   |Place=             Brighouse
   |Map =              Huddersfield_-_Kirklees_dot.png
   |Population =       32,198 (according to 2001 census)
   |District=          [[Calderdale]]
   |County=            [[West Yorkshire]]
   |Region=            [[Yorkshire and the Humber]]
   |Ceremonial=        [[West Yorkshire]]
   |Traditional=       [[Yorkshire]]
   |Constituency=      [[Calder Valley (UK Parliament constituency)|Calder Valley]]
   |Police=            [[West Yorkshire Police|West Yorkshire]]
   |PostalTown=        HUDDERSFIELD
   |PostCode=          HD6
   |DiallingCode=      01484
   |GridReference=     SE135235
   |Euro=              [[Yorkshire and the Humber (European Parliament constituency)|Yorkshire and the Humber]]
 }}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
[[Image:BrighouseCrest.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Brighouse Coat of Arms]]

'''Brighouse''' is the second largest town in the metropolitan district of [[Calderdale]] in the [[county]] of [[West Yorkshire]], [[England]].  The [[2001]] [[census]] gave the town's population as 32,198.

The town is situated on the [[River Calder]], 4 [[mile|miles]] (6 [[kilometer|km]]) east of [[Halifax, West Yorkshire|Halifax]] in the [[Pennines]]. It is served by Junction 25 of the [[M62 motorway|M62]] and its own [[Brighouse railway station|railway station]] on the [[Caldervale line]].

The name Brighouse (or &quot;Bridge House&quot;) originates from a building on (or close to) the bridge over the [[River Calder]]. In its early history, it was a [[hamlet (place)|hamlet]] of the nearby town of [[Rastrick]].

==Architecture==
Two churches in Brighouse have [[stained glass]] from the [[William Morris]] factory, designed by [[Dante Gabriel Rossetti]], [[Ford Madox Brown]] and [[Edward Burne-Jones]].

==History==
In [[prehistoric]] times, there was a [[ford (river)|ford]] called Snake Hill Ford across the Calder - this was part of the [[Roman Britain|Roman]] route between [[Wakefield]] and [[Manchester]]. 

A wooden structure called Rastrick [[Bridge]] was recorded as being present in [[1275]].  The bridge was replaced by one built with [[timber]] donated by John Hanson in [[1514]].  Hanson's son funded a stone replacement for this bridge in [[1558]].  

The river provided power for the [[flour]] milling industry and the [[textile]] mills. Brighouse's industry received a boost through the [[construction]] of the [[Calder and Hebble Navigation]], started in [[1757]] by the [[engineer]] [[John Smeaton]].

The Halifax and Huddersfield Turnpike Act of [[1823]] allowed for the building of Calder Bridge over what was to become the A641 road ; tolls were abolished on the bridge in [[1875]] and extensive widening work in was undertaken in [[1905]] and [[1999]] (both of these latter dates being commemorated in dedication stones on the bridge).

==Brighouse and Rastrick Brass Band==
Brighouse is the home of the well-known and almost chart-topping '''[[Brighouse and Rastrick Brass Band]]''', founded in [[1881]] and associated also with Brighouse's neighbour [[Rastrick]].  They originally formed under the name ''The Brighouse and Rastrick Temperance Brass Band'' and took their current title in the early [[20th century]].

The band won the National Brass Band Contest in [[1946]] at the [[Royal Albert Hall]] and in [[1968]] they won the first Brass Band World Championship.  

In [[1977]] they recorded ''The Floral Dance'' which reached Number 2 in the UK Top 40 charts and stayed there for 9 weeks, inspiring [[radio]] [[DJ]] [[Terry Wogan]] to release his own version some time later.

In [[1993]] they became the first [[Yorkshire]] team to win the All England Masters Brass Band Championship, a feat they to were repeat in [[1998]].  The band also won the National Brass Band Championship Finals in [[1997]] and [[1998]], and the European Championships in [[1998]].

==Brighouse Library and Smith art gallery==
Brighouse library is a huge building and has many services, such as:

*Adult and children's lending
*Wheelchair access
*Language courses
*Internet access
*Fax facillities

The Smith art gallery shares premises with the library. There are many paintings from the victorian era located in the main hall,  there is also a changing program of exhibitions of artwork from local artists.

==Sport==
===Football===
During the 2003-4 season, the town's football club, Brighouse Town A.F.C. played in the West Riding County Amateur Football League Premier Division at level 12 of the [[National League System]] (also known as 'The Football Pyramid').

==Fish And Chips==
One of the main fish and chip shops in central Brighouse is one known as ''The Happy Haddock''. It gained some fame from [[Tony Blair]] visiting there for some fish and chips during the 2001 election campaign. The vinegar he got on his hands from this caused him to have to wipe them on his trousers before he could shake hands with members of the public[http://news.bbc.co.uk/vote2001/hi/english/newsid_1338000/1338709.stm].

Another popular fish and chip shop in Brighouse is the ''Golden Hind'', it also produces southern fried chicken.

==Nearby Villages and suburbs==
Brookfoot, Baliff Bridge, [[Clifton, West Yorkshire|Clifton]], Hove Edge,Hipperholme, Lightcliffe, [[Rastrick]]
==Buildings and renovations==
At the moment most of Brighouse is in need of renovations including a few buildings that are disused. Most buildigs which have been renovated make the place look a lot better like the new Prego restraunt and hotel. There are also a few eyesores such as Sugdens Mill which is getting knocked down to make way for a new swimming pool/sports centre. There are also many shops on Commercial Street such as Woolworths, Choices and Just Books.

==External links==
* [http://www.brighouseandrastrickband.co.uk/ Brighouse and Rastrick Brass Band]
* [http://www.btafc.com/ Brighouse Town AFC site]
* [http://www.brighousetoday.co.uk/ ''Brighouse Today'' - Online news provided by the ''Brighouse Echo'' newspaper]
* [http://www.brighouseonline.com/ Brighouse Online - A site full of information about Brighouse with guides, shops, maps etc]
* [http://www.bardelang.f2s.com/chess/brighousechess.htm Brighouse Chess Club]
* [http://neighbourhood.statistics.gov.uk/AreaProfileFrames.asp?aid=177249&amp;hid=14&amp;tid=14 Census data]

